2. Formal Problem Setup

2.1 Query space and resource budgets

Let \mathcal{X} be a measurable space of queries, equipped with a probability distribution \mathcal{D} (the deployment distribution). The system can allocate a budget b \in \mathcal{B} \subset \mathbb{R}^k to each query, where \mathcal{B} is a compact set of feasible resource vectors (e.g., maximum token count, number of decoding passes). For a fixed budget b, a policy is a Markov kernel \pi_b : \mathcal{X} \to \Delta(\mathcal{T}) that maps a query to a distribution over reasoning traces \tau \in \mathcal{T}, where \mathcal{T} is a Polish space of token sequences. The stochasticity models both the autoregressive sampling and any additional randomness of the reasoning protocol.

2.2 Quality and cost functions

From a trace \tau an answer y is extracted, and its quality is evaluated by a measurable function Q(y,x) \in [0,1]. The expected quality of budget b on query x is

\tilde{Q}(b,x) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi_b}[Q(y(\tau), x)].

We assume \tilde{Q}(\cdot, x) is continuous for each x; this follows if \pi_b is weakly continuous (Feller) and Q is bounded continuous. The trace also incurs measurable costs: token count T(\tau), wall‑clock latency L(\tau), and energy E(\tau). Normalise by dataset medians \bar{T}, \bar{L}, \bar{E} from a reference fixed‑budget policy. Given user‑specified weights \alpha,\beta,\gamma \ge 0 with \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1, the normalised additive cost of a trace is

C(\tau) = \alpha \frac{T(\tau)}{\bar{T}} + \beta \frac{L(\tau)}{\bar{L}} + \gamma \frac{E(\tau)}{\bar{E}}.

The expected cost of budget b on query x is

C(b,x) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi_b}[C(\tau)].

2.3 Randomised policies and the primal problem

The decision maker can randomise over budgets. A randomised policy is a Markov kernel \pi : \mathcal{X} \to \Delta(\mathcal{B}). For each query, the policy samples b \sim \pi(\cdot|x) and then generates a trace according to \pi_b. The expected quality and cost of such a policy are

\mathcal{Q}(\pi) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \tilde{Q}(b,x) \, \pi(db \mid x) \, \mathcal{D}(dx), \qquad
\mathcal{C}(\pi) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \int_{\mathcal{B}} C(b,x) \, \pi(db \mid x) \, \mathcal{D}(dx).

The system must respect an average resource budget B_{\max} > 0. The primal problem is

P^* = \sup_{\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}}} \mathcal{Q}(\pi) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{C}(\pi) \le B_{\max}. \tag{2.1}

Here \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}} is the space of all such Markov kernels. Allowing randomisation convexifies the problem; a deterministic policy mapping each x to a single budget is a special case (a Dirac kernel), but the supremum over deterministic policies may be strictly smaller than P^*.

