3. Lagrangian Duality and Budget Targeting

3.1 Lagrangian and dual function

Introduce a Lagrange multiplier \lambda \ge 0 for the budget constraint. The Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda) = \mathcal{Q}(\pi) - \lambda \bigl( \mathcal{C}(\pi) - B_{\max} \bigr).

The dual function is obtained by maximising over \pi:

g(\lambda) = \sup_{\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}}} \mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda)
= \int_{\mathcal{X}} \max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \bigl[ \tilde{Q}(b,x) - \lambda C(b,x) \bigr] \, \mathcal{D}(dx) + \lambda B_{\max}, \tag{3.1}

where we have interchanged supremum and integral using the Carathéodory nature of the integrand and compactness of \mathcal{B} [Rockafellar & Wets, 1998, Theorem 14.60].

3.2 Measurable optimal budget selection

For each query x and shadow price \lambda, define the set of optimal budgets

\mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) = \arg\max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \bigl[ \tilde{Q}(b,x) - \lambda C(b,x) \bigr].

This correspondence is non‑empty, compact‑valued, and upper hemicontinuous because \tilde{Q} and C are continuous and \mathcal{B} is compact. To obtain a single measurable selector, we use a lexicographic tie‑breaking rule: primary, maximise \tilde{Q} - \lambda C; secondary, minimise the expected cost C(b,x). Specifically,

b^*(x;\lambda) = \arg\min_{b \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda)} C(b,x). \tag{3.2}

To guarantee uniqueness of the minimiser, we assume either that \mathcal{B} is finite, or that C(\cdot, x) is strictly convex and \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) is convex (which holds if \tilde{Q} is strictly concave). Under either condition, b^*(x;\lambda) is a Borel‑measurable function by the Aumann measurable selection theorem [Aumann, 1965] and the stability of lexicographic minima.

3.3 Strong duality

The primal problem (2.1) is an infinite‑dimensional linear program over a convex set. Strong duality holds under a Slater condition: there exists a strictly feasible randomised policy. We assume \exists \bar b \in \mathcal{B} such that \sup_{x} C(\bar b, x) < B_{\max} (e.g., a zero‑cost ‘do nothing’ action). Then [Shapiro, 2001, Theorem 2.1] guarantees

P^* = D^* := \inf_{\lambda \ge 0} g(\lambda),

and the infimum is attained at some \lambda^* \ge 0. The optimal randomised policy for the primal is given by the kernel that puts probability one on b^*(x;\lambda^*) for almost all x.

Define the expected cost of the \lambda-optimal policy:

C^*(\lambda) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} C\bigl( b^*(x;\lambda), x \bigr) \, \mathcal{D}(dx).

Lemma 3.1 (Monotonicity). If \lambda_1 < \lambda_2, then C^*(\lambda_2) \le C^*(\lambda_1).

Proof. Fix x. Let b_i = b^*(x;\lambda_i). The optimality conditions give

\tilde{Q}(b_1) - \lambda_1 C(b_1) \ge \tilde{Q}(b_2) - \lambda_1 C(b_2),\qquad
\tilde{Q}(b_2) - \lambda_2 C(b_2) \ge \tilde{Q}(b_1) - \lambda_2 C(b_1).

Adding and rearranging yields (\lambda_2 - \lambda_1)(C(b_1)-C(b_2)) \ge 0, so C(b_1) \ge C(b_2). The result follows by taking expectation over x. ∎

3.4 Right‑continuity of C^*

To locate \lambda^* by binary search we need continuity. The tie‑breaking rule yields right‑continuity:

Lemma 3.2. Under the strict convexity assumption (or the finite \mathcal{B} case), C^* is right‑continuous on [0,\infty).

Proof. Fix \lambda and let \lambda_n \downarrow \lambda. Berge’s maximum theorem gives upper hemicontinuity of \mathcal{A}(x,\cdot). Because \mathcal{B} is compact, the primary objective is continuous, and the secondary minimiser is unique (by strict convexity), the selector b^*(x;\lambda_n) converges pointwise to b^*(x;\lambda). By dominated convergence, C^*(\lambda_n) \to C^*(\lambda). ∎

3.5 Sensitivity and binary search complexity

To quantify how fast a binary search over \lambda can reach a target budget, we need a modulus of continuity for C^*. Suppose \tilde{Q}(\cdot,x) is \mu-strongly concave and L_Q-Lipschitz, while C is L_C-Lipschitz. Then the unique maximiser b^*(\lambda) is Lipschitz in \lambda with constant L_Q/\mu [Bonnans & Shapiro, 2000, Theorem 4.51]. Consequently, C^* is Lipschitz with constant L_\lambda = L_C L_Q / \mu.

If strong concavity fails, one still obtains Hölder continuity of order 1/2. In either case, there exist \alpha \in (0,1] and L_\lambda > 0 such that

|C^*(\lambda_1) - C^*(\lambda_2)| \le L_\lambda |\lambda_1 - \lambda_2|^\alpha.

A binary search on [0,\Lambda] (with \Lambda large enough that C^*(\Lambda) < B_{\max}) halves the interval each step. To guarantee |C^*(\hat\lambda) - B_{\max}| \le \delta, the final \lambda-interval width must satisfy L_\lambda (\Delta \lambda)^\alpha \le \delta. Hence the number of iterations is

O\!\left( \log_2 \frac{\Lambda}{\delta^{1/\alpha} / L_\lambda^{1/\alpha}} \right) = O(\log(1/\delta)),

with a constant that deteriorates as \alpha \to 0. This establishes:

Theorem 3.1 (Budget Targeting). Under the Slater condition and the sensitivity assumptions, there exists a unique shadow price \lambda^* such that the randomised policy using b^*(\cdot;\lambda^*) achieves the primal optimum. Moreover, binary search on \lambda returns a policy with expected cost within \delta of B_{\max} in O(\log(1/\delta)) steps.

