4. Regret Bound for Difficulty Estimation

The planner requires the true query difficulty, which is not directly observable. We define difficulty operationally and bound the loss from using an estimator.

4.1 Operational definition of difficulty

Fix a reference shadow price \lambda_0 (the operating point). For each query x, define the intrinsic difficulty as the expected cost incurred by the optimal budget under \lambda_0:

d^*(x) = C\bigl( b^*(x;\lambda_0), x \bigr). \tag{4.1}

Since C is bounded, d^*(x) \in [d_{\min}, d_{\max}]. This definition connects difficulty directly to resource consumption at a well‑defined optimal trade‑off, and it is measurable because b^* is measurable.

4.2 Planner as a Hölder map in Wasserstein space

In practice, budgets are discrete (e.g., token counts 1,2,\dots,K). A deterministic map from estimated difficulty \hat d to a specific budget cannot be Hölder continuous unless it is constant. To restore smoothness, we allow the planner to output a randomised budget: a distribution over \mathcal{B}. Let \psi : \mathbb{R} \to \Delta(\mathcal{B}) be a Markov kernel, with \Delta(\mathcal{B}) equipped with the 1‑Wasserstein distance W_1. We assume \psi is (M,h)-Hölder:

W_1\bigl( \psi(d_1), \psi(d_2) \bigr) \le M |d_1 - d_2|^h, \qquad h \in (0,1]. \tag{4.2}

This holds, for instance, if \psi(d) is a softmax over bins with smoothly varying logits.

Given a budget distribution p \in \Delta(\mathcal{B}), the expected quality is \tilde{Q}(p,x) = \mathbb{E}_{b \sim p}[\tilde{Q}(b,x)]. If \tilde{Q}(\cdot,x) is L_Q-Lipschitz on \mathcal{B}, then for any p_1, p_2,

|\tilde{Q}(p_1,x) - \tilde{Q}(p_2,x)| \le L_Q \, W_1(p_1, p_2) \tag{4.3}

by Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality [Villani, 2009]. The oracle policy uses the true difficulty d^*(x) and obtains budget distribution \psi(d^*(x)); the estimated policy uses \psi(\hat d(x)).

4.3 Hölder regret bound

The expected regret is

\mathcal{R} = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}} \Bigl[ \tilde{Q}\bigl(\psi(d^*(x)), x\bigr) - \tilde{Q}\bigl(\psi(\hat d(x)), x\bigr) \Bigr].

Applying (4.2) and (4.3) yields

\mathcal{R} \le L_Q M \, \mathbb{E}_x \bigl[ |d^*(x) - \hat d(x)|^h \bigr]. \tag{4.4}

Let \epsilon = \mathbb{E}_x[|d^*(x) - \hat d(x)|] be the mean absolute error. Since z \mapsto z^h is concave for h \in (0,1], Jensen’s inequality gives

\mathbb{E}[|d^* - \hat d|^h] \le \epsilon^h.

Thus we obtain the deterministic bound

\mathcal{R} \le L_Q M \, \epsilon^h. \tag{4.5}

4.4 PAC‑Bayesian generalisation guarantee

A difficulty estimator is learned from a finite training set \mathcal{S} = \{(x_i, d^*(x_i))\}_{i=1}^m drawn i.i.d. from \mathcal{D}. We adopt a stochastic predictor: a posterior distribution \rho over a hypothesis class \mathcal{H} of functions \phi: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}. For each query, we sample \phi \sim \rho and predict \hat d_\phi(x). The expected error is \epsilon(\rho) = \mathbb{E}_{\phi \sim \rho}\mathbb{E}_x[|\hat d_\phi(x) - d^*(x)|]. The absolute error is bounded by D = d_{\max} - d_{\min}.

Using a modern PAC‑Bayesian bound for bounded loss [Alquier, 2024], for any fixed prior \pi over \mathcal{H}, with probability at least 1-\delta over \mathcal{S},

\epsilon(\rho) \le \hat\epsilon_\rho + D \sqrt{ \frac{ \operatorname{KL}(\rho \| \pi) + \log \frac{2\sqrt{m}}{\delta} }{2m} }, \tag{4.6}

where \hat\epsilon_\rho = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^m \mathbb{E}_{\phi \sim \rho}[|\hat d_\phi(x_i) - d^*(x_i)|]. The regret of the randomised policy now becomes \mathcal{R}(\rho) = \mathbb{E}_{\phi \sim \rho} \mathcal{R}_\phi. By convexity of the loss and Jensen’s inequality, the bound (4.5) extends:

\mathcal{R}(\rho) \le L_Q M \, \bigl( \epsilon(\rho) \bigr)^h.

Combining with (4.6) gives the final high‑probability regret guarantee:

Theorem 4.1 (PAC‑Bayesian Regret Bound). Under the Lipschitz and Hölder assumptions, with probability 1-\delta over the training sample, the policy using the stochastic difficulty predictor \rho satisfies

\mathcal{R}(\rho) \le L_Q M \left( \hat\epsilon_\rho + D \sqrt{ \frac{ \operatorname{KL}(\rho \| \pi) + \log \frac{2\sqrt{m}}{\delta} }{2m} } \right)^{\!h}.

This bound explicitly quantifies how improvements in difficulty calibration translate into lower policy suboptimality.

