5. POMDP Model and Optimal Stopping

The solver–verifier loop that actually generates tokens and decides when to halt is modelled as a partially observable Markov decision process.

5.1 POMDP formulation

The POMDP consists of:

· State space \mathcal{S}: a Polish space of latent reasoning states (e.g., logical completeness, correctness).
· Observations \mathcal{O}: generated token sequences, along with predictive entropy H_t and log‑probabilities.
· Actions \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{V} \cup \{\texttt{HALT}\}, where \mathcal{V} is the vocabulary.
· Transition kernel T: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \Delta(\mathcal{S}): weakly continuous.
· Observation kernel Z: \mathcal{S} \to \Delta(\mathcal{O}).
· Reward: R(s,a) = \Delta\text{Prog}(s,a) - \eta for a \in \mathcal{V} (\eta > 0 is a token penalty), and terminal reward R(s,\texttt{HALT}) = Q(\text{answer}, x).
· Horizon: finite, H = \lceil Q_{\max} / \eta \rceil, because after H tokens the cumulative penalty exceeds the maximum possible quality, making further continuation suboptimal.

The agent does not observe the true state; it maintains a belief b_t \in \Delta(\mathcal{S}), updated recursively via Bayes’ rule. Under mild regularity (weak continuity of T and Z), the belief‑state process is a fully observable MDP with state space \Delta(\mathcal{S}) [Smallwood & Sondik, 1973].

5.2 Finite‑horizon Bellman equation

Let V_t^*(b) be the optimal value from belief b at time t. For the finite horizon H, the Bellman equation is

V_{H+1}^*(b) = 0, \qquad
V_t^*(b) = \max \Bigl\{
\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{s \sim b}[Q(s)]}_{\text{halt}},
\max_{a \in \mathcal{V}} \bigl( \mathbb{E}_{s \sim b}[R(s,a)] + \mathbb{E}_{o \sim Z, a}[ V_{t+1}^*(\tau(b,a,o)) ] \bigr)
\Bigr\},

where \tau(b,a,o) is the belief update after taking action a and observing o.

Define the continuation value

Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{V}} \Bigl( \mathbb{E}_{s \sim b}[R(s,a)] + \mathbb{E}_{o}[ V_{t+1}^*(\tau(b,a,o)) ] \Bigr). \tag{5.1}

The optimal decision at belief b at time t is to halt if and only if

\mathbb{E}_{s \sim b}[Q(s)] \ge Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b). \tag{5.2}

5.3 Monotonicity and confidence‑threshold stopping

To obtain a simple, interpretable stopping rule we impose structural assumptions. First, we equip \mathcal{S} with a partial order \preceq that reflects progress: s \preceq s' means s' is more advanced, and the terminal reward Q(s) is non‑decreasing in s. Second, we assume the transition and observation kernels preserve the monotone likelihood ratio (MLR) order: if belief b is MLR‑dominated by b', then after any action and observation the updated beliefs maintain that dominance. This property holds when T and Z have log‑supermodular densities [Shaked & Shanthikumar, 2007]; it is a strong assumption but serves as a sufficient condition. Third, we assume diminishing returns: the incremental value of continuing, Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) - \mathbb{E}_b[Q], is non‑increasing as the belief moves up the MLR order.

Under these conditions, \mathbb{E}_b[Q] is increasing in the MLR order, and Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) is decreasing. Define the confidence measure

\text{Conf}(b) = 1 - \frac{H(b)}{H_{\max}}, \qquad H_{\max} = \log|\mathcal{V}|,

where H(b) is the model’s predictive entropy when in belief b. We further assume that higher MLR beliefs correspond to lower entropy, so that \text{Conf} is strictly increasing in the MLR order.

Then the difference \Delta^{(t)}(b) = \mathbb{E}_b[Q] - Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) is strictly increasing in confidence. Because the belief space is connected and the value functions are continuous, the set where \Delta^{(t)}(b) \ge 0 is an interval. Thus, for each time step t there exists a threshold \theta_t^* such that the optimal policy halts iff \text{Conf}(b_t) \ge \theta_t^*. The thresholds are non‑increasing in t because the value of continuing decreases as the horizon shrinks.

Theorem 5.1 (Confidence‑Threshold Stopping). Under the MLR dominance and diminishing returns assumptions, the optimal halting policy for the finite‑horizon POMDP is a threshold rule on confidence: halt at the first time t where \text{Conf}(b_t) \ge \theta_t^*. Without these assumptions, the optimal stopping region is still characterised by the inequality (5.2).

This result provides a rigorous foundation for entropy‑gated halting heuristics: they are optimal if the underlying process satisfies the monotonicity conditions, and even when those conditions are violated, the Bellman‑derived condition (5.2) remains the principled criterion.

