7. Conclusion

We have constructed a mathematically rigorous framework for adaptive inference in large language models. The Lagrangian dual planner with measurable optimal budget map and strong duality guarantees provably optimal budget allocation under a global resource constraint. The regret bound, strengthened by PAC‑Bayesian generalisation, explicitly connects the quality of a difficulty estimator to the performance of the resulting adaptive policy—a link that has been missing in prior heuristic methods. The POMDP analysis of the solver–verifier loop yields an optimal stopping condition that, under sufficient monotonicity assumptions, reduces to a simple confidence threshold, providing a theoretical basis for entropy‑gated generation. Finally, the additive Efficiency Intelligence Score avoids the variance pitfalls of multiplicative metrics and is properly calibrated against a baseline.

Together, these results establish ERA not merely as an engineering system but as a verifiable theory of resource‑aware inference. The theorems are ready to guide the design and evaluation of next‑generation language models that must balance intelligence with efficiency.


Appendix A. Lagrangian Dual and Measurable Selection

A.1 Space of Randomised Policies and Convexity

Let \mathcal{X} be a measurable space with probability measure \mathcal{D}. The budget space \mathcal{B} \subset \mathbb{R}^k is compact. A randomised policy is a Markov kernel \pi : \mathcal{X} \to \Delta(\mathcal{B}), where \Delta(\mathcal{B}) is the set of Borel probability measures on \mathcal{B} equipped with the topology of weak convergence. The set \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}} of all such kernels is convex: for any \pi_1,\pi_2 \in \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}} and \alpha \in [0,1], the mixture \pi = \alpha\pi_1 + (1-\alpha)\pi_2 defined pointwise by \pi(\cdot|x) = \alpha\pi_1(\cdot|x) + (1-\alpha)\pi_2(\cdot|x) is again a Markov kernel.

The expected quality and cost are linear functionals on \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}}. Indeed, for any x, the map p \mapsto \int_{\mathcal{B}} \tilde{Q}(b,x) p(db) is linear and weakly continuous (since \tilde{Q} is bounded continuous). Integration over \mathcal{D} preserves linearity. Thus \mathcal{Q} : \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}} \to \mathbb{R} and \mathcal{C} : \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}} \to \mathbb{R} are linear functionals, and the primal problem

P^* = \sup\{ \mathcal{Q}(\pi) : \pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}},\; \mathcal{C}(\pi) \le B_{\max} \}

is a linear program in the infinite-dimensional space of measures.

A.2 Strong Duality

Define the Lagrangian \mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda) = \mathcal{Q}(\pi) - \lambda (\mathcal{C}(\pi) - B_{\max}) for \lambda \ge 0. The dual function is g(\lambda) = \sup_{\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{rand}}} \mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda). Because the supremum of an affine functional over a convex set can be exchanged with the integral under mild conditions (Rockafellar & Wets, 1998, Theorem 14.60), we obtain

g(\lambda) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \sup_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \bigl[ \tilde{Q}(b,x) - \lambda C(b,x) \bigr] \mathcal{D}(dx) + \lambda B_{\max}.

Strong duality holds under the Slater condition: there exists a strictly feasible policy. We assume there is a budget \bar b \in \mathcal{B} such that \sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} C(\bar b, x) < B_{\max}. The constant policy \pi_0(\cdot|x) = \delta_{\bar b} then satisfies \mathcal{C}(\pi_0) \le \sup_x C(\bar b, x) < B_{\max}. By standard results for linear programming in paired spaces (Shapiro, 2001, Theorem 2.1), the primal and dual values coincide: P^* = \inf_{\lambda \ge 0} g(\lambda), and the infimum is attained at some \lambda^* \ge 0.

A.3 Measurable Selection of Optimal Budget

For each \lambda \ge 0 and x \in \mathcal{X}, define the objective f_\lambda(b,x) = \tilde{Q}(b,x) - \lambda C(b,x). The map (b,x) \mapsto f_\lambda(b,x) is a Carathéodory function (continuous in b, measurable in x). The argmax correspondence

\mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) = \arg\max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} f_\lambda(b,x)

is nonempty, compact-valued, and upper hemicontinuous in x (by Berge's maximum theorem, since \mathcal{B} is compact and f_\lambda continuous). To obtain a single-valued measurable selector, we impose a secondary criterion: among all maximisers, choose the one that minimizes C(b,x). We assume either:

1. \mathcal{B} is finite, or
2. C(\cdot,x) is strictly convex on \mathcal{B} and \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) is convex (the latter holds if \tilde{Q}(\cdot,x) is concave, as the argmax of a concave function is convex).

Under either condition, the minimisation of C over \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) has a unique solution. Define

b^*(x;\lambda) = \arg\min_{b \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda)} C(b,x).

The map (x,\lambda) \mapsto b^*(x;\lambda) is a Borel function. This follows from the Aumann measurable selection theorem (Aumann, 1965): the argmax correspondence admits a measurable selector; the secondary selection (min of a continuous function over a measurable compact-valued correspondence) is again measurable (see Aliprantis & Border, 2006, Chapter 18).

A.4 Monotonicity of Expected Cost

Define C^*(\lambda) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} C(b^*(x;\lambda), x) \mathcal{D}(dx).

Lemma A.1. If \lambda_1 < \lambda_2, then C^*(\lambda_2) \le C^*(\lambda_1).

Proof. Fix x and set b_i = b^*(x;\lambda_i). Optimality gives

\tilde{Q}(b_1) - \lambda_1 C(b_1) \ge \tilde{Q}(b_2) - \lambda_1 C(b_2),

\tilde{Q}(b_2) - \lambda_2 C(b_2) \ge \tilde{Q}(b_1) - \lambda_2 C(b_1).

Adding and rearranging yields (\lambda_2 - \lambda_1)(C(b_1)-C(b_2)) \ge 0. Since \lambda_2 > \lambda_1, we obtain C(b_1) \ge C(b_2). The inequality is preserved by expectation over x. ∎

A.5 Right-Continuity of C^*(\lambda)

Lemma A.2. Under the uniqueness assumption above, C^* is right-continuous.

Proof. Let \lambda_n \downarrow \lambda. For each x, consider the sequence b_n = b^*(x;\lambda_n). Since \mathcal{B} is compact, there is a convergent subsequence b_{n_k} \to \bar{b}. Upper hemicontinuity of \mathcal{A}(x,\cdot) implies \bar{b} \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda). The primary objective satisfies

f_{\lambda_{n_k}}(b_{n_k},x) \ge f_{\lambda_{n_k}}(b,x) \quad \forall b \in \mathcal{B}.

Taking limits and using continuity of f in both arguments, we get f_\lambda(\bar{b},x) \ge f_\lambda(b,x) for all b, hence \bar{b} \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda). Now consider the secondary criterion. For any b' \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda), by upper hemicontinuity of \mathcal{A} there exists a sequence b'_n \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda_n) with b'_n \to b'. Since b_n minimizes C over \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda_n),

C(b_n,x) \le C(b'_n,x).

Taking limits, C(\bar{b},x) \le C(b',x). As this holds for all b' \in \mathcal{A}(x,\lambda) and the minimizer is unique, \bar{b} = b^*(x;\lambda). Thus the entire sequence b_n converges pointwise to b^*(x;\lambda). Dominated convergence then yields C^*(\lambda_n) \to C^*(\lambda). ∎

