Appendix B. Sensitivity Analysis and Convergence of Binary Search

B.1 Lipschitz Dependence under Strong Concavity

Assume that for every x, \tilde{Q}(\cdot,x) is \mu-strongly concave on the convex set \mathcal{B}: for all b_1,b_2 \in \mathcal{B}, \alpha \in [0,1],

\tilde{Q}(\alpha b_1 + (1-\alpha)b_2, x) \ge \alpha \tilde{Q}(b_1,x) + (1-\alpha)\tilde{Q}(b_2,x) + \frac{\mu}{2}\alpha(1-\alpha)\|b_1-b_2\|^2.

Also assume \tilde{Q} and C are L_Q- and L_C-Lipschitz respectively. Then the function b \mapsto \tilde{Q}(b,x) - \lambda C(b,x) is strongly concave with parameter \mu, and its gradient (if differentiable) is Lipschitz. By Bonnans & Shapiro (2000, Theorem 4.51), the unique maximizer b^*(x;\lambda) is Lipschitz in \lambda with constant L_C/\mu. Hence

|C(b^*(x;\lambda_1),x) - C(b^*(x;\lambda_2),x)| \le L_C \|b^*(x;\lambda_1) - b^*(x;\lambda_2)\| \le \frac{L_C^2}{\mu} |\lambda_1 - \lambda_2|.

Taking expectation over x gives

|C^*(\lambda_1) - C^*(\lambda_2)| \le L_\lambda |\lambda_1 - \lambda_2|, \qquad L_\lambda = \frac{L_C^2}{\mu}.

B.2 Hölder Case

Without strong concavity, the maximizer is typically Hölder continuous of order 1/2 under second-order sufficient conditions. Then there exists K such that \|b^*(\lambda_1) - b^*(\lambda_2)\| \le K |\lambda_1 - \lambda_2|^{1/2}. Consequently, C^* is Hölder of order 1/2 with constant L_C K.

B.3 Binary Search Complexity

Monotonicity and right-continuity imply that the set \{\lambda \ge 0 : C^*(\lambda) \le B_{\max}\} is an interval [\lambda^*, \infty). We can locate \lambda^* by bisection on [0,\Lambda], where \Lambda is chosen so that C^*(\Lambda) < B_{\max}. In each iteration, we estimate C^* at the midpoint \lambda using a validation set.

Assume we have a continuity modulus |C^*(\lambda_1) - C^*(\lambda_2)| \le L |\lambda_1 - \lambda_2|^\alpha for some \alpha \in (0,1]. After k iterations, the remaining interval length is \Lambda/2^k. The budget error is bounded by L (\Lambda/2^k)^\alpha. To guarantee this is \le \delta, we need

k \ge \log_2\left( \frac{\Lambda}{(\delta/L)^{1/\alpha}} \right).

Thus the number of iterations is O(\log(1/\delta)), with the constant deteriorating as \alpha \to 0. This completes the proof of the budget targeting theorem.

