Appendix C. Regret Bound with Difficulty Estimation: Full Proof

C.1 Definition of True Difficulty

Let \lambda_0 > 0 be a reference shadow price. For each query x, define

d^*(x) = C( b^*(x;\lambda_0), x ).

Because C is bounded, d^*(x) \in [d_{\min}, d_{\max}] =: [0,D], where we set d_{\min}=0 without loss. The function d^* is measurable since b^* is measurable.

C.2 Wasserstein Planner and Lipschitz Quality

Let \Delta(\mathcal{B}) be endowed with the 1-Wasserstein distance

W_1(p_1,p_2) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(p_1,p_2)} \int_{\mathcal{B}\times\mathcal{B}} \|b_1-b_2\| \, \gamma(db_1,db_2).

A planner \psi: \mathbb{R} \to \Delta(\mathcal{B}) is (M,h)-Hölder if W_1(\psi(d_1),\psi(d_2)) \le M |d_1-d_2|^h for all d_1,d_2. The expected quality of a distribution p \in \Delta(\mathcal{B}) is \tilde{Q}(p,x) = \int_{\mathcal{B}} \tilde{Q}(b,x) p(db). Since \tilde{Q}(\cdot,x) is L_Q-Lipschitz on \mathcal{B}, the Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality yields

|\tilde{Q}(p_1,x) - \tilde{Q}(p_2,x)| \le L_Q \, W_1(p_1,p_2).

C.3 Deterministic Regret Bound

The oracle policy draws budget from \psi(d^*(x)), the estimated policy from \psi(\hat d(x)). The regret is

\mathcal{R} = \mathbb{E}_x \bigl[ \tilde{Q}(\psi(d^*(x)), x) - \tilde{Q}(\psi(\hat d(x)), x) \bigr].

Applying the Hölder and Lipschitz bounds:

\mathcal{R} \le L_Q \mathbb{E}_x \bigl[ W_1(\psi(d^*(x)), \psi(\hat d(x))) \bigr] \le L_Q M \mathbb{E}_x\bigl[ |d^*(x) - \hat d(x)|^h \bigr].

Let \epsilon = \mathbb{E}_x[|d^*(x) - \hat d(x)|]. The function t \mapsto t^h is concave for h \in (0,1], so by Jensen's inequality,

\mathbb{E}_x[|d^* - \hat d|^h] \le (\mathbb{E}_x[|d^* - \hat d|])^h = \epsilon^h.

Hence

\mathcal{R} \le L_Q M \epsilon^h.

This inequality is deterministic, holding for any given estimator \hat d.

C.4 PAC-Bayesian Bound for Stochastic Estimator

We now consider a distribution \rho over a hypothesis class \mathcal{H} of predictors. The stochastic predictor draws \phi \sim \rho and returns \hat d_\phi(x). The expected error is \epsilon(\rho) = \mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} \mathbb{E}_x |\hat d_\phi(x) - d^*(x)|. The empirical error on a sample \mathcal{S} = \{(x_i, d^*(x_i))\}_{i=1}^m is \hat\epsilon_\rho = \frac1m \sum_{i=1}^m \mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} |\hat d_\phi(x_i) - d^*(x_i)|. The loss is bounded by D = d_{\max} - d_{\min}.

The PAC-Bayesian theorem (Alquier, 2024) states: for any prior \pi on \mathcal{H}, with probability at least 1-\delta over \mathcal{S},

\epsilon(\rho) \le \hat\epsilon_\rho + D \sqrt{ \frac{ \operatorname{KL}(\rho\|\pi) + \log\frac{2\sqrt{m}}{\delta} }{2m} }.

Now consider the stochastic policy: first sample \phi \sim \rho, then for each query use planner \psi(\hat d_\phi(x)). Its expected regret is

\mathcal{R}(\rho) = \mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} \mathbb{E}_x \bigl[ \tilde{Q}(\psi(d^*(x)), x) - \tilde{Q}(\psi(\hat d_\phi(x)), x) \bigr].

By the same Lipschitz/Hölder argument applied inside the expectation over \phi,

\mathcal{R}(\rho) \le L_Q M \, \mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} \mathbb{E}_x [ |d^*(x) - \hat d_\phi(x)|^h ].

Now use the fact that for any non-negative random variable Z with mean \mu, \mathbb{E}[Z^h] \le \mu^h (by Jensen since t\mapsto t^h is concave). Apply with Z = \mathbb{E}_x |d^*(x) - \hat d_\phi(x)|, whose expectation over \phi is \epsilon(\rho). Thus

\mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} \mathbb{E}_x [ |d^* - \hat d_\phi|^h ] \le \bigl( \mathbb{E}_{\phi\sim\rho} \mathbb{E}_x |d^* - \hat d_\phi| \bigr)^h = \epsilon(\rho)^h.

Therefore \mathcal{R}(\rho) \le L_Q M \, \epsilon(\rho)^h. Combining with the PAC-Bayes bound yields the final high-probability statement:

\mathcal{R}(\rho) \le L_Q M \left( \hat\epsilon_\rho + D \sqrt{ \frac{ \operatorname{KL}(\rho\|\pi) + \log\frac{2\sqrt{m}}{\delta} }{2m} } \right)^{\!h}.

