Appendix D. POMDP and Optimal Stopping: Detailed Proofs

D.1 Finite-Horizon POMDP and Belief-MDP Reduction

We consider a POMDP \langle \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, T, Z, R, H \rangle where \mathcal{S} is a Polish state space, \mathcal{O} the observation space, \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{V} \cup \{\texttt{HALT}\}, T and Z are weakly continuous transition/observation kernels, and R bounded measurable. The horizon H = \lceil Q_{\max}/\eta \rceil ensures that continuing beyond H is suboptimal because total token penalty would exceed the maximum reward.

Belief b_t \in \Delta(\mathcal{S}) is a conditional probability over the state given history. The belief update operator \tau: \Delta(\mathcal{S}) \times \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{O} \to \Delta(\mathcal{S}) is defined via Bayes rule. Under weak continuity of T and Z, the map (b,a,o) \mapsto \tau(b,a,o) is continuous. The belief-MDP (\Delta(\mathcal{S}), \mathcal{A}, \tilde{T}, \tilde{R}) with

\tilde{R}(b,a) = \int_{\mathcal{S}} R(s,a) b(ds), \qquad \tilde{T}(\cdot | b,a) = \int_{\mathcal{O}} \delta_{\tau(b,a,o)}(\cdot) \, Z(do|s) b(ds)

is a fully observable MDP on the space of belief measures, which is a Borel space (Smallwood & Sondik, 1973). Standard dynamic programming yields optimal value functions V_t^* \in C_b(\Delta(\mathcal{S})) satisfying

V_{H+1}^*(b) = 0, \quad V_t^*(b) = \max\bigl\{ \tilde{R}(b,\texttt{HALT}),\, \max_{a\in\mathcal{V}} [ \tilde{R}(b,a) + \mathbb{E}_{b'\sim \tilde{T}(\cdot|b,a)} V_{t+1}^*(b') ] \bigr\}.

D.2 Continuation Value and Optimal Stopping

Define the continuation value

Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{V}} \bigl( \tilde{R}(b,a) + \mathbb{E}_{b'} [ V_{t+1}^*(b') ] \bigr).

The optimal action at belief b and time t is \texttt{HALT} iff

\tilde{R}(b,\texttt{HALT}) \ge Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b). \tag{D.1}

Since \tilde{R}(b,\texttt{HALT}) = \mathbb{E}_{s\sim b}[Q(s)], this is exactly the inequality in the text.

D.3 Sufficient Conditions for a Confidence-Threshold Policy

We now impose structural assumptions to simplify (D.1) to a threshold on confidence.

Partial order on \mathcal{S}. Let \preceq be a closed partial order representing progress. The terminal reward Q: \mathcal{S} \to [0,1] is non-decreasing: s \preceq s' \Rightarrow Q(s) \le Q(s').

MLR order. For \mu,\nu \in \Delta(\mathcal{S}), we say \mu \le_r \nu (MLR order) if the Radon–Nikodym derivative d\nu/d\mu is monotone non-decreasing with respect to \preceq. This is a stochastic order implying first-order stochastic dominance.

Assumption D.1 (MLR-preserving transitions). For any action a \in \mathcal{V} and observation o, the map b \mapsto \tau(b,a,o) is monotone with respect to the MLR order: b \le_r b' \Rightarrow \tau(b,a,o) \le_r \tau(b',a,o). A sufficient condition is that T and Z have densities that are totally positive of order 2 (TP2) in the state variable (Shaked & Shanthikumar, 2007).

Assumption D.2 (Diminishing returns). The function b \mapsto \Delta^{(t)}(b) = \mathbb{E}_b[Q] - Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) is non-decreasing in the MLR order; i.e., if b \le_r b', then \Delta^{(t)}(b) \le \Delta^{(t)}(b').

Confidence. Define the confidence measure \text{Conf}(b) = 1 - H(b)/H_{\max} where H(b) = -\sum_{y} p(y|b) \log p(y|b) is the predictive entropy of the next token given belief b (marginalising over state). We assume that the MLR order correlates with lower entropy: b \le_r b' implies \text{Conf}(b) \le \text{Conf}(b').

Lemma D.1. Under Assumptions D.1 and D.2, the function b \mapsto \mathbb{E}_b[Q] is non-decreasing in MLR order, and b \mapsto Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) is non-increasing.

Proof. The first part follows because MLR implies first-order stochastic dominance and Q is increasing. The second part is exactly the diminishing returns assumption: \mathbb{E}_b[Q] - Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)}(b) increasing implies Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)} decreases relative to \mathbb{E}_b[Q]; a more precise statement is that Q_{\text{cont}}^{(t)} itself is non-increasing in MLR order (one can prove by backward induction using the monotonicity of the Bellman operator under TP2 conditions; see Lovejoy, 1987). ∎

Consequently, \Delta^{(t)}(b) is strictly increasing in MLR order. Since confidence is assumed to be monotone in MLR, \Delta^{(t)}(b) is strictly increasing in \text{Conf}(b). The optimal halting condition \Delta^{(t)}(b) \ge 0 therefore corresponds to \text{Conf}(b) \ge \theta_t^* for some threshold \theta_t^* (which may depend on t). The thresholds are non-increasing in t because the continuation value decreases as horizon shrinks.

This completes the proof of Theorem 5.1 (confidence-threshold stopping).

