Appendix E. Efficiency Intelligence Score: Variance Stability and Scale‑Consistency

E.1 Additive Cost and Variance Inflation of Multiplicative Metrics

We work under the collinearity model

L = k_1 T + \varepsilon_1, \qquad E = k_2 T + \varepsilon_2,

with \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i]=0, \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_i) < \infty, \mathbb{E}[T^4] < \infty, and \varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2 independent of T and of each other.

Additive cost.

C = \alpha \frac{T}{\bar T} + \beta \frac{k_1 T + \varepsilon_1}{\bar L} + \gamma \frac{k_2 T + \varepsilon_2}{\bar E}
= \underbrace{ \left( \frac{\alpha}{\bar T} + \frac{\beta k_1}{\bar L} + \frac{\gamma k_2}{\bar E} \right) }_{A} T + \frac{\beta}{\bar L}\varepsilon_1 + \frac{\gamma}{\bar E}\varepsilon_2.

Then \operatorname{Var}(C) = A^2 \operatorname{Var}(T) + \frac{\beta^2}{\bar L^2}\operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_1) + \frac{\gamma^2}{\bar E^2}\operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_2) = O(\operatorname{Var}(T)).

Multiplicative metric M = T \cdot L \cdot E.

M = T (k_1 T + \varepsilon_1)(k_2 T + \varepsilon_2) = k_1 k_2 T^3 + k_1 T^2 \varepsilon_2 + k_2 T^2 \varepsilon_1 + T \varepsilon_1 \varepsilon_2.

Using independence and zero mean of \varepsilon_i,

\operatorname{Var}(M) = k_1^2 k_2^2 \operatorname{Var}(T^3) + k_1^2 \mathbb{E}[T^4] \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_2) + k_2^2 \mathbb{E}[T^4] \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_1) + \mathbb{E}[T^2] \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_1)\operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_2).

The dominant term is \operatorname{Var}(T^3). Expand T = \bar T + \Delta with \mathbb{E}[\Delta]=0:

T^3 = \bar T^3 + 3\bar T^2 \Delta + 3\bar T \Delta^2 + \Delta^3.

Thus \mathbb{E}[T^3] = \bar T^3 + 3\bar T \sigma_T^2 + \mathbb{E}[\Delta^3] and

\operatorname{Var}(T^3) = \mathbb{E}[ (3\bar T^2 \Delta + 3\bar T \Delta^2 + \Delta^3 - 3\bar T \sigma_T^2 - \mathbb{E}[\Delta^3] )^2 ].

The leading term comes from (3\bar T^2 \Delta)^2, giving 9\bar T^4 \sigma_T^2. All remaining terms involve higher moments of \Delta and are O(\bar T^3 \mathbb{E}[|\Delta|^3]) which is controlled by the finite fourth moment. Therefore,

\operatorname{Var}(M) \approx 9 k_1^2 k_2^2 \bar T^4 \operatorname{Var}(T) + O(\bar T^3).

Comparing to \operatorname{Var}(C) = A^2 \operatorname{Var}(T) + \text{const}, we see the multiplicative metric’s variance is amplified by a factor of order \bar T^4, demonstrating its instability.

E.2 EIS Properties: Calibration and Scale‑Consistency

EIS for a trace \tau is \text{EIS}(\tau) = \frac{Q \cdot \text{Conf}}{1 + C(\tau)} \kappa, where \kappa is chosen so that the baseline policy \pi_0 has expected EIS of 1 on validation set \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}.

Boundedness: Q,\text{Conf} \in [0,1], C \ge 0 implies numerator \le 1 and denominator \ge 1, so 0 \le \text{EIS} \le \kappa. The maximum is attained only at perfect confidence, perfect quality, and zero cost.

Non‑degeneracy: If a model produces trivial answers with T \approx 0 but Q small, denominator \approx 1, score \le \kappa \cdot Q \cdot \text{Conf} is finite, preventing inflation.

Scale‑consistency: When cost weights are scaled by c > 0, the new cost is C' = cC. The new calibration constant is \kappa_c = \left( \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}} \bigl[ \frac{Q_0 \cdot \text{Conf}_0}{1 + c C_0} \bigr] \right)^{-1}. For any model, expected EIS under the new scaling is \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \frac{Q \cdot \text{Conf}}{1 + c C} \bigr] \kappa_c. The ranking between two models is determined by the sign of

\mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{Q_A \text{Conf}_A}{1 + c C_A} \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{Q_B \text{Conf}_B}{1 + c C_B} \right],

multiplied by \kappa_c > 0. This difference is a function of c. In general, the ordering can change with c, reflecting the intended sensitivity to cost. However, if one model stochastically dominates the other (i.e., its cost is stochastically smaller and its quality‑confidence product stochastically larger), then the difference remains positive for all c. The metric is thus scale‑consistent: the baseline stays calibrated, and changes in ranking only occur when the cost scaling genuinely alters the trade‑off. This is a desirable property rather than a flaw.

